References: <SsCntWZjuGI.v3kp_p2VBgAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/SsCntWZjuGI/m/tVHq0PSzBgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Hannah,

FWIW: Link (1999) offers some perspective <http://www.jstor.org/stable/3802817>.   I would think that if p-star is really high for cattle or people, using psi-hat cow or psi-hat person (or observed cow or human presence for that matter) might be reasonably approximate.  Not to betray 'unmarked' or anything, but you can fit MacKenzie's 2004 model (and Richmond's more stable 2010 model) in MARK/PRESENCE, so if that's the sort of model you want to evaluate, you may not need to be approximate.

That said, there are plenty of examples where a detection index for one species is used as a predictor for another.

