Hi Jacob,

If you haven't looked into it yet, you can view how the mb.gof() test in the AICcmodavg package approaches the order of NAs in a detection history. Of note, however, is that Marc M. recently undertook what I can only imagine was a pretty major overhaul of his code to address two missing data issues possibly (if rarely) arising in a multi-season data set (1. a site not visited in a year and 2. a year without any repeated surveys; thanks again, Marc!). However, even though the offending dataset was very sparse, I formatted the observations so that NAs were always trailing.

Generally, it seems that coding surveys via "round" (creating the issue you describe when missing surveys are peppered about) is done when other, more informative covariates are missing (like survey date), though I suppose in some cases "round" could act as a surrogate for something else important suspected of affecting detection probabilities. I suspect "rounds" are employed as detection covariates in occupancy models more often than one might think (though I don't have numbers) as this is a standard detection-component setting available in PRESENCE. I don't, however, know how PRESENCE approaches the cohort issue you describe.

Giancarlo

